problem "An ecologist found that the growth of the number of frogs in a wetland is directly proportional to the difference between the wetland’s maximum frog population and the current population. Let $F(t)$ be the frog population, measured in number of frogs, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum frog population the wetland can support is $232$ frogs, which differential equation could be used to model the frog population? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality."] [:db/add "322e7971-29aa-50c5-a6d3-ea7d6622a62d" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the frog population $F(t)$ of the wetland changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum frog population the wetland can support and the current frog population. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-F),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum frog population the wetland can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=232\\text{ frogs},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k(232-F).\n$$"] [:db/add "322e7971-29aa-50c5-a6d3-ea7d6622a62d" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] [:db/add "322e7971-29aa-50c5-a6d3-ea7d6622a62d" :question/answer-fields "ed5fafe2-a4fe-5096-b57d-62e35de2b3cf"] [:db/ensure "322e7971-29aa-50c5-a6d3-ea7d6622a62d" :question/validate] [:db/add 17592186124674 :knowledge-point/questions "322e7971-29aa-50c5-a6d3-ea7d6622a62d"] [:db/add "e5eb74f9-313b-5dda-b150-454e5979e820" :answer/id #uuid "e5eb74f9-313b-5dda-b150-454e5979e820"] [:db/add "e5eb74f9-313b-5dda-b150-454e5979e820" :answer/type :answer.type/math] [:db/add "e5eb74f9-313b-5dda-b150-454e5979e820" :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(P-79)"] [:db/ensure "e5eb74f9-313b-5dda-b150-454e5979e820" :answer/validate] [:db/add "88812519-c775-5836-85bb-df3d56d55c46" :answer/id #uuid "88812519-c775-5836-85bb-df3d56d55c46"] [:db/add "88812519-c775-5836-85bb-df3d56d55c46" :answer/type :answer.type/math] [:db/a